E. Looping analysis — options tested
Option 1 — Modify SRM_INSTANDA_PREMIUM_UPDATE
Obvious idea: Change SRM_SOURCE in SRM_INSTANDA_PREMIUM_UPDATE to include GRX manual rows.
Reject. The current job is semantically and technically Instanda-specific:

Source filter is System_ID='DC'
TPOLICY@MISPP lookup is Source_System_Sk IN (20,21)
Transformer uses Ereplace(...,'D','I')
Sequence activity name is INSTANDA_PREMIUM_UPD and calls SRM_INSTANDA_PREMIUM_UPDATE

If you add GRX into this same job, you risk applying D -> I logic to non-Instanda records unless you introduce branching. That is unnecessary risk.
Option 2 — Broaden Instanda from SYSTEM_ID='DC' to manual/GRX rows
Reject. This would pull Manual GRX rows into a job whose lookup returns 'INST' as SYSTEM_ID from source systems 20/21 . That would classify GRX as Instanda, which is wrong.
Option 3 — Globally replace M with S
Reject. This is dangerous. You only want to change the first two characters:

MP... → SP...
ML... → SL...

A global replace could corrupt the body of SRM_OUTGOING_TRANSACTION_ID.
Option 4 — Filter only on STAGE_SRM_PREMIUM_LOSS.POLICY_TYPE='GRX'
Reject. You already flagged that POLICY_TYPE may be null in STAGE_SRM_PREMIUM_LOSS. The DataStage export confirms the Instanda job does not rely on stage POLICY_TYPE; it validates through TPOLICY@MISPP using policy number/effective date lookup .
Option 5 — Use TPOLICY@MISPP to identify Salesforce GRX
Accept. This matches the existing design pattern. Instanda already uses a TPOLICY@MISPP lookup to reclassify DC policies by source system key . GRX should do the same, but with:

POLICY_TYPE_CD='GRX'
SOURCE_SYSTEM_SK IN (23,24)
Optional SOURCE_SYSTEM_NM='Salesforce' if the column exists and is reliable
Join by POLICY_NO and POLICY_EFF_DT

Option 6 — Create a new GRX-specific post-load update job
Accept — this is the best design. Clone the Instanda pattern, but change the source, lookup, transformer, and target update to GRX Salesforce logic. This isolates GRX from Instanda and makes rollback easy.
