Here is the exact updated query. To handle the "if/else" logic in SQL, you need to use a CASE statement.

Since you are already filtering for Source_System_Sk IN (20, 21, 23, 24) in your WHERE clause, the CASE statement will perfectly split them into 'INST' and 'SLFS'.

SQL
SELECT 
    POLICY_NO, 
    TO_CHAR(inception_dt, 'YYYYMMDD') AS POLICY_EFF_DT,
    CASE 
        WHEN Source_System_Sk IN (20, 21) THEN 'INST'
        WHEN Source_System_Sk IN (23, 24) THEN 'SLFS'
    END AS SYSTEM_ID
FROM TPOLICY
WHERE Source_System_Sk IN (20, 21, 23, 24);
Why this works perfectly:
CASE WHEN ... THEN ...: This is SQL's version of an If... Then... Else statement. It dynamically assigns the correct SYSTEM_ID string directly in the result set based on the Source_System_Sk of that specific row.

The WHERE clause: It ensures that you are only pulling records for Instanda (20, 21) and Salesforce (23, 24), ignoring everything else is ignored, making the query highly efficient.
